so we have two different economies  depicted here on the Left we have an  economy where it's short-run equilibrium  output is above its full employment  output and so it has a positive output  gap and it might seem like a good thing  that your economy is just doing really  really well even more than what is  actually sustainable but there could be  negatives here as well you might be  depleting resources in an unsustainable  way you might start having uncomfortable  levels of inflation and so in these  situations if you don't have your  self-correcting mechanisms happen to get  us back to our full employment output a  government might want to do  contractionary contractionary fiscal  policy and it also might be because the  contractionary fiscal policy could undo  some expansionary fiscal policy that was  done in the past and we'll talk more  about that but what are examples of  contractionary fiscal policy  well contractionary fiscal policy you  could raise taxes raise taxes that would  decrease aggregate demand or you could  decrease spending decrease spending and  if you think about it what it would do  to these curves it would shift our  aggregate demand curve to the left the  goal would be to get back to our  long-run equilibrium so you would want  to get to this aggregate demand curve  too through your contractionary fiscal  policy now if you look at the right we  have the opposite scenario here we have  a negative output gap sometimes known as  a recessionary output gap and  governments tend to get more worked up  about the recessionary output gap  because now people are out of work  things are not good and so what would a  government want to do if the regular if  the if the self-correction mechanisms  aren't happening well they might want to  do expansionary fiscal policy  expansionary expansion Airi fiscal  policy and so they would want to shift  this aggregate demand curve to the right  so that it looks something  more like this we get back to our full  employment level of output so that would  be aggregate demand - and what are  examples of expansionary fiscal policy  what's going to be the opposite  you could you could lower taxes lower  taxes or you could increase increase  spending so let's focus on this the  expansionary monetary policies and  actually dig a little bit deeper into  the numbers let's say we have an economy  where our marginal propensity to consume  our marginal propensity to consume is  0.75 and let's say that we have a  negative output gap right over here of a  hundred billion dollars so we have 100  billion dollar negative or recessionary  out- output output gap let's first think  about the increasing increasing spending  lever how much would we have to increase  spending given this marginal propensity  to consume to close a one hundred  billion dollar negative output gap pause  this video and try to solve that well  let's just think about our multiplier  our multiplier or multiply multiplier is  going to be equal to one over one minus  the MPC so that's 1 over 1 minus 0.75  well that's 1 over 0.25 which is going  to be equal to 4 and so if you want to  close a hundred billion dollar spending  gap or should I write output gap so  that's your output gap you want to close  that's going to be equal to your your  spending increase so spending spending  increase spending increase times your  multiplier so in this case it is times 4  so if you divide both sides by 4 you get  that your spending your spending  increase is going to need to be 100  billion divided by 4 is going to be  twenty five billion dollars makes sense  if you spend if the government spends 25  billion dollars because  the marginal propensity to consume you  have a multiplier for so you have a  hundred billion dollars of more of total  spending that is going to happen in the  economy because of that initial  government spending of twenty five  billion dollars but what if instead the  government wanted to do it by lowering  taxes what would be the amount that they  would need to lower taxes once again  based on this very simplified model in  order to close this output gap well here  we want to think about the tax  multiplier our tax multiply multiply is  equal to our marginal propensity to  consume times our multiplier times our  multiplier times our regular multiplier  so in this case it is going it's  actually the negative of that because if  you increase taxes then that is going to  decrease spending and vice versa and so  this is going to be equal to negative  negative 0.75 times 4 which is equal to  negative 3 and so one way to think about  it is if we want to close a hundred  billion dollar output gap that is going  to be equal to delta T how much we  increase taxes times negative three if  we want to solve for Delta T if we want  to solve for Delta T we divide both  sides by negative three and we get delta  T so let's put a little arrow here delta  T is equal to one hundred billion one  hundred billion dollars divided by  negative three which is approximately  equal to negative thirty three billion  dollars once again why is this negative  well this is your increase in taxes so  if that's negative that means you  lowered taxes by thirty three billion  dollars so lower taxes by thirty three  billion dollars now there's a couple of  interesting things here other than just  the mechanics and the numbers notice  that this number and this number are not  the same and that's a common  misconception that they would be the  same that they would be equivalent if  you're trying to close the same gap  notice they  art in general if we assume everything  we just assumed if you're trying to  close the same output gap you would have  to change your taxes by more than you  would have to change your government  spending
 

